7] Hyperlink: docs/DEVELOPER_SETUP.md @middle-center", "[248] Hyperlink: Permalink: Quick Start @middle-left", "[249] ListItem: Say \"Pulse\" and wait for it to respond. @middle-center", "[250] ListItem: Say what you want, in plain language \u2014 e.g. \"Open Notepad and write 'hello, how  @middle-center", "[251] ListItem: Listen (or read the overlay) as Pulse narrates each step. @middle-center", "[252] ListItem: If Pulse needs a detail it doesn't have (a filename, a location), it'll ask \u2014 ju @middle-center", "[253] Hyperlink: docs/QUICKSTART.md @bottom-left", "[254] Hyperlink: Permalink: \ud83e\uddb8 Superhero Mode @bottom-left", "[255] Button: Copy code to clipboard @bottom-center", "[256] Hyperlink: Permalink: Roadmap @bottom-left", "[257] ListItem: A published, downloadable installer release (buildable from source today \u2014 see I @bottom-center", "[258] Hyperlink: Installation @bottom-center", "[259] ListItem: A first-run download-progress UI for the installer's one-time ~6GB model fetch @bottom-center", "[260] ListItem: Optional launch-at-startup @bottom-center", "[261] ListItem: Broader, more reliable app coverage (this is where your bug reports matter most) @bottom-center", "[262] Hyperlink: Permalink: Known Limitations @bottom-left", "[263] ListItem: Windows only. No macOS/Linux support yet. @bottom-center", "[264] ListItem: No published installer release yet. A packaged installer is buildable from sourc @bottom-center", "[265] Hyperlink: Installation @bottom-center", "[266] Hyperlink: Roadmap @bottom-center", "[267] ListItem: Quality depends on the local model. Planning runs on a small, locally-hosted mod @bottom-center", "[268] ListItem: Apps with unusual save/session behavior can still confuse it. Cloud-autosaving a @bottom-center", "[269] ListItem: Vision fallback is a real capability, not infallible. It can misread low-contras @bottom-center", "[270] ListItem: No sandboxing. Pulse automates your real desktop through the same UI-Automation  @bottom-center", "[271] Hyperlink: Open an issue @bottom-left", "[272] Hyperlink: Permalink: Contributing @bottom-left", "[273] Hyperlink: CONTRIBUTING.md @bottom-center", "[274] Hyperlink: docs/DEVELOPER_SETUP.md @bottom-left", "[275] Hyperlink: CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md @bottom-center", "[276] Hyperlink: Known Limitations @bottom-center", "[277] Hyperlink: Permalink: Privacy @bottom-left", "[278] Hyperlink: Permalink: Accessibility @bottom-left", "[279] Hyperlink: Superhero Mode @bottom-center", "[280] Hyperlink: open an issue @bottom-center", "[281] Hyperlink: Permalink: Built with Open Technologies @bottom-left", "[282] Hyperlink: Permalink: License @bottom-left", "[283] Hyperlink: PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0 @bottom-center", "[284] Hyperlink: LICENSE @bottom-center", "[285] Button: Edit repository metadata @top-right", "[286] Hyperlink: Readme @top-right", "[287] Hyperlink: License @top-right", "[288] Hyperlink: Code of conduct @top-right", "[289] Hyperlink: Contributing @top-right", "[290] Hyperlink: Security policy @top-right", "[291] Hyperlink: Activity @top-right", "[292] Hyperlink: 0 stars @top-right", "[293] Hyperlink: 0 watching @top-right", "[294] Hyperlink: 0 forks @top-right", "[295] Hyperlink: Releases @top-right", "[296] Hyperlink: Create a new release @top-right", "[297] Hyperlink: Packages @top-right", "[298] Hyperlink: Publish your first package @top-right", "[299] Hyperlink: Contributors @top-right", "[300] ListItem: aman2003s @top-right", "[301] Hyperlink: @aman2003s @top-right", "[302] Hyperlink: aman2003s @top-right", "[303] Hyperlink: More workflows @top-right", "[304] Button: Dismiss suggestions @top-right"], "visible_text": ["Repository navigation", "Public", "aman2003s/pulse-ai", "had recent pushes 5 minutes ago", "feature/fast-response-optimization", "Add file", "This branch is", "main", "Folders and files", "Latest commit", "History", "Repository files navigation", "Pulse", "Control your computer through natural conversation.", "\u2014 see the interface, the pitch, and the demo video in one place.", "Pulse is a", "local, voice-first AI computer companion", ". Say \"Pulse,\" describe what you want to do, and Pulse understands your intent, performs the task on your actual desktop, and keeps you informed the entire time \u2014 no cloud round-trip, no account, no shortcuts to memorize.", "You:   \"Pulse, open Notepad and write 'hello, how are you'\"\nPulse: \"Opening Notepad. Typing that in now. Done.\"", "Designed with accessibility at its core, and built to be simple enough for anyone. Pulse is not an assistant that", "complements", "a screen reader \u2014 it's meant to let someone drive their whole computer by voice, full stop.", "This is an early, honest preview", ", not a finished product. It's public so people can install it, try it, break it, and tell us what's wrong \u2014 see", "before you judge it too harshly.", "Table of Contents", "Why Pulse Exists", "Modern computers expect users to learn shortcuts, menus, commands, and complex interfaces before getting real work done. For many people \u2014 especially those with visual impairments or limited mobility \u2014 those barriers turn everyday tasks into obstacles.", "Pulse changes the interaction model: instead of learning how to use software, you describe what you want to accomplish, and Pulse figures out how.", "How It Works", "Wake", "\"I'm listening.\"", "Understand", "\"Open my electricity bill,\"", "\"Launch Chrome,\"", "\"Find my resume.\"", "Plan", "Execute", "Confirm", "Under the hood, this is a real reason \u2192 act \u2192 observe \u2192 re-plan loop: Pulse states what it expects to happen next, checks that against what actually happened, and corrects course itself when reality doesn't match \u2014 rather than blindly executing a fixed script. See", "for the technical shape of this.", "Features", "Core Principles", "Accessibility First", "Voice First", "Local & Private", "Human Control", "What Pulse Can Do Today", "Screenshots", "A short launch demo is available at", ". Static screenshots of the individual UI states are still needed \u2014 see", "for exactly what's needed if you'd like to help.", "Architecture", "Pulse is split into a thin, disposable", "UI layer", "and a headless", "Core Engine", "that owns all the actual intelligence \u2014 voice, planning, task state, and execution. The UI talks to the Core over a local WebSocket API and has no logic of its own; you could swap it out entirely without touching how Pulse thinks or acts.", "observed result", "progress", "state/events", "\ud83c\udf99\ufe0f Microphone", "Wake Word", "Speech-to-Text", "Conversation Manager", "AI Planner", "(local LLM)", "Task Manager", "Execution Controller", "Tool Engine", "Windows Adapter", "\ud83d\udda5\ufe0f Operating System", "Text-to-Speech", "Tauri UI", "(thin overlay)", "Full detail \u2014 component responsibilities, the command-processing loop, the tool/observer/safety model, and startup sequence \u2014 lives in", ". Product philosophy and rationale live in", "and", "Stack", ": Python core (planner client, task manager, tool executor, Windows UI-Automation adapter) \u00b7", "serving a local Gemma model for planning and vision \u00b7 Whisper-family STT \u00b7 Kokoro TTS \u00b7 a", "(Rust + web) overlay for the UI, talking to the core over a local WebSocket (", ").", "Installation", "Pulse is Windows-only today.", "Packaged installer", ": build one yourself with", "cd ui/src-tauri && cargo tauri build", "(needs", "cargo install tauri-cli --version \"^2\"", "first) \u2014 this produces a single", ".exe", "that installs the full app (UI + backend), no admin rights needed. First launch downloads the model weights (~6GB) automatically; see", "for exactly what's verified and what's still rough (no download-progress UI yet, wake-word retraining stays source-only). A prebuilt release isn't published yet \u2014 see the repo's", "page.", "Run from source", "(the tested, complete path today):", "Clone the repo", "git clone https://github.com/aman2003s/pulse-ai.git", "cd", "pulse-ai", "Set up the Python environment", "python -m venv venv\nvenv", "\\S", "cripts", "\\a", "ctivate\npip install -e", "Fetch the local models", "python scripts/fetch_models.py", "Run it", "run.bat", "ui/pulse-ui.exe", "Full step-by-step instructions, hardware expectations, and troubleshooting live in", "and", ". Setting up a dev environment to contribute code is covered in", "Quick Start", "Once Pulse is running:", "\"Pulse\"", "\"Open Notepad and write 'hello, how are you'\"", "See", "for more example commands and what to expect from each.", "\ud83e\uddb8 Superhero Mode", "For users who want continuous guidance rather than quiet automation. Most software goes silent once an action starts \u2014 Superhero Mode keeps Pulse narrating: typing, navigation, loading states, and other progress that would otherwise pass silently.", "You:   \"Pulse, enable Superhero Mode.\"\nPulse: \"Superhero Mode activated. I'll guide you through everything I do.\"\nYou:   \"Open my Downloads folder.\"\nPulse: \"Opening Downloads. I'm reading the folder contents. I found 24 items\n        including Documents, Images, and Videos. What would you like to do", "Roadmap", "Built incrementally, with each feature tested against real, messy, real-world apps before moving on.", "Current foundation", ": voice interaction, wake word, local AI planning, on-screen understanding (text + visual fallback), multi-step task execution with self-correction, accessibility-tuned feedback.", "Near-term", "Future", ": browser automation, developer workflows, cross-platform support, a plugin/skill ecosystem, richer screen understanding.", "Known Limitations", "Read this before filing \"it doesn't work\" \u2014 some of these are real, open problems, not surprises:", "Windows only.", "No published installer release yet.", "Quality depends on the local model.", "Apps with unusual save/session behavior can still confuse it.", "Vision fallback is a real capability, not infallible.", "No sandboxing.", "Found something not listed here?", "\u2014 that's exactly what this release is for.", "Contributing", "Contributions happen through pull requests only \u2014 see", "for the full workflow,", "to get a dev environment running, and", "for how we expect people to treat each other here.", "Not sure where to start? Small, well-scoped bug reports and fixes are the most valuable thing right now \u2014 see", "above.", "Privacy", "Pulse's planning, speech recognition, and text-to-speech run on local models on your machine. Voice audio and screen content are processed locally to decide what to do \u2014 they are not sent to a cloud AI provider as part of Pulse's own operation. Some tools (e.g. web search) necessarily make outbound ", "Accessibility", "Accessibility is not a feature layered on top of Pulse \u2014 it's the reason Pulse exists. It's built for people with visual impairments or limited mobility to operate a full Windows desktop by voice, and tuned (via", "and configurable feedback levels) to keep users continuously informed rather than leaving them guessing what's happening. If something about Pulse gets in the way of that goal, please", "\u2014 accessibility reports get priority.", "Built with Open Technologies", "Pulse is powered by open technologies: local language models, speech recognition, text-to-speech, and wake-word detection. The project exists to demonstrate that capable AI assistants can be built while respecting user privacy and keeping users in control.", "License", "Pulse is licensed under the", "\u2014 free for personal use, education, research, and contributions. Commercial use requires permission from the licensor. See", "for the full terms.", "\u00a9 2026 Pulse Project", "About Edit repository metadata", "Pulse is a local, voice-first AI computer companion that helps people accomplish computer tasks naturally. Simply say \"Pulse\", describe what you want to do, and Pulse understands your intent, performs the task, and keeps you informed throughout the entire process. Designed with accessibility at its ", "Resources", "Code of conduct", "Contributing", "Security policy", "Stars", "Watchers", "Forks", "Releases", "No releases published", "Packages", "No packages published", "Contributors \u00a0(1)", "Languages", "Suggested workflows", "Based on your tech stack"], "verified": true}, {"success": true, "app": "word", "path": "C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\Word.lnk", "already_running": true, "verified": true}]
Compare the REAL results above against what you expected â€” set expectation_met accordingly next round. These are things YOU just did, not things the user or anyone else did â€” narrate them as 'I did X' / 'I've typed X', never as 'I see X is already there'. Continue only if more steps are genuinely needed â€” use the REAL results above (actual file paths, actual numbered elements from a read_screen if one just ran; if you need to interact with something and haven't read the screen since it last changed, read it again first). If a step failed, decide whether to retry differently or tell the user what went wrong instead of pretending it worked. If the goal is fully complete, return an empty plan and a brief closing 'speak' confirming what you did â€” do not repeat something you already said this task.
