2014 Built a voice-answer test harness per direction (Kokoro TTS synthes @bottom-right", "[342] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 Retested the code-driven backstop live: it correctly forced the que @bottom-right", "[343] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 User caught a real gap live: read_screen showed [1] Button: OK (a \" @bottom-right", "[344] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 Implemented and live-verified the hard-timeout fix for capture_unti @bottom-right", "[345] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 Root-caused the Word-focus failures from the last several tests: th @bottom-right", "[346] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 Implemented the TVAE (Thinking-Verification-Action-Expectation) pat @bottom-right", "[347] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 TVAE live results (Word end-to-end): the trial dialog that broke th @bottom-right", "[348] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 Root-caused exactly why the model wouldn't click either visible \"Cl @bottom-right", "[349] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 Addressed directly (\"limit only when one step is repeated many time @bottom-right", "[350] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 Per direction (\"check the actual input/output, think why it's happe @bottom-right", "[351] ListItem: 2026-07-27 \u2014 The prior fix's retry condition didn't cover everything: the very n @bottom-right", "[352] ListItem: 2026-07-28 \u2014 Live-tested the exact Word notepad-equivalent case end to end (\"ope @bottom-right", "[353] ListItem: 2026-07-28 \u2014 HTN redesign implementation completed: _execute_task_list/_act_obse @bottom-right", "[354] ListItem: 2026-07-28/29 \u2014 Live end-to-end Word test run (three iterations, same goal each  @bottom-right"], "visible_text": ["Aman", "Pro", "Claude is responding", "Claude responded: Research done.", "I need to strip out all the limits that are causing failures, fix the bugs like the restart loop and missing task list population, and ensure the AI returns a complete list that gets processed incrementally rather than being truncated or depth-limited.\n\nWait, there's a tension here\u2014they said to reve", "So this confirms my hypothesis about the reasoning-action disconnect\u2014it's a documented issue where the model's reasoning doesn't align with its structured output. The solution is to validate the output against the reasoning context, and if it fails validation, retry with the specific error message f", "I found the registry key that permanently disables cloud-first saving across all Office applications\u2014setting `PreferCloudSaveLocations` to 0 in the Common\\General folder. This is a much more reliable solution than repeatedly navigating the UI preferences, and it applies uniformly to Word, Excel, and", "Research", "done.", "Here's", "what", "found", "and", "the", "plan", "I'd", "propose", "confirming", "with", "you", "before", "touching", "any", "code.", "1. Why", "task_list", "keeps silently disappearing:", "this is a known, named failure mode \u2014 the \"reasoning-action disconnect.\" Models reliably say", "\"this needs a task_list\"", "in their reasoning while still emitting a JSON blob that omits the field, because nothing forces the two to agree. The standard fix (used across production agent frameworks): validate the output, and if a compound-looking goal comes back without a real", "task_list", ", retry that same call once with the mismatch quoted back at it (\"you said this needs a breakdown but didn't include one \u2014 return it now\"), capped at a few attempts,", "not silently proceeding on the broken", "response", "like", "today.", "2. Word's cloud-save default is a known, fixable Office setting, not something to fight in the UI every time.", "All Office apps (Word/Excel/PowerPoint) share one registry value:", "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Office\\16.0\\Common\\General\\PreferCloudSaveLocations", ". Setting it to", "once permanently makes \"Save\"/\"Save As\" default to a local folder instead of OneDrive, for every Office app, for good. That's a one-time fix at the root instead of fragile per-save dialog navigation,", "and it directly answers \"our app should understand the save block\" \u2014 Pulse can check/set this itself rather", "than", "needing", "UI", "workaround.", "3. Replacing the round-based \"limits\":", "the", "consecutive_no_progress", "mechanism (nudge at round 5, park at round 9) is what actually caused Tuesday's restart bug \u2014 its own wording (\"step back, is there a fundamentally different way to approach this?\") is what the model misread as permission to restart. Your instinct is right: that whole mechanism should go. In its pla", "save_file", "\u2014 if the tool's own error says it needs a custom multi-step flow (already a distinct signal it returns today), decompose into sub-steps; otherwise treat it as a plain retry, up to", "3 attempts, and only after all 3 fail, report that honestly. No", "round-count", "budget", "acting", "as", "behavioral", "nudge", "the", "50-round", "ceiling", "stays", "only", "as", "true", "last-resort", "runaway", "guard", "that", "should", "never", "fire", "on", "real", "work.", "Proposed", "changes,", "concretely:", "consecutive_no_progress", "NO_PROGRESS_HINT_AT", "NO_PROGRESS_PARK_AT", "_act_observe", "PreferCloudSaveLocations", "save_verify_failures", "Want me to go ahead with all four?", "11 minutes ago", "You said: test first and then implement and i want logs and flow again", "test first and then implement and i want logs and flow again", "9 minutes ago", "Claude responded: All verified \u2014 Office 16.", "The user wants me to test the current state first to gather baseline logs and flow information before implementing any fixes, then proceed with the implementation while continuing to provide those logs and flow details. So the right approach is to first verify my assumptions about the Office setup o", "I've confirmed the Office registry hive is correct for this installation, and the `PreferCloudSaveLocations` setting isn't currently configured, which means it's using the default behavior that favors cloud locations for new documents in modern Office versions\u2014explaining why Word is defaulting to cl", "Both registry keys are currently unset, defaulting to cloud preferences. I'll set PreferCloudSaveLocations to 0 as the main fix, while EnableCloudOnlySaveAsMode doesn't need adjustment since it's not enforced and likely isn't the culprit here. Let me check the registry schema in the plan next.", "I need to examine the full schema including the required fields to get a complete picture of the current structure.", "So `task_list` being optional in the schema means the grammar-constrained generation doesn't force the model to include it \u2014 it can validly omit it entirely, which explains why the model skipped it even though its reasoning suggested it should be there.\n\nI see two paths forward: either make `task_li", "I'm checking the rest of this function to see if there's already handling for custom save dialogs, since I noticed a comment indicating awareness that some applications replace the standard Windows dialog with their own.", "I see there's already a specific error message for when the save dialog appears but the file doesn't actually get written to disk\u2014that's distinct from the case where no dialog opens at all. These are two different failure modes that need separate handling.\n\nNow with the registry fix to disable cloud", "All verified \u2014 Office 16.0 hive exists on this machine,", "PreferCloudSaveLocations", "is currently unset (defaulting to cloud), and the schema confirms", "task_list", "is optional (which is exactly why the model can silently drop it). Implementing now.", "Now wiring this into", "pulse.py", "'s startup sequence:", "Now I'm looking at the current `_run_task_loop` implementation in controller.py to add validation and retry logic for the task list.", "Now let's add the", "task_list", "validation+retry in", "_run_task_loop", "I'm setting up a heuristic to detect compound goals by counting distinct action verbs in the goal text. I'll create a regex pattern that matches common action verbs like \"open,\" \"write,\" \"type,\" \"save,\" and \"send\" as whole words, then use this to determine if a goal involves multiple distinct action", "Now adding the", "_looks_compound", "helper method:", "I should clean this up by adding a proper `import re` statement at the top of the file instead of using that inline import hack, but first I need to check if `re` is already imported at the module level.", "I'm deciding between two approaches: either add a proper module-level `import re` and use a clean class constant, or keep the file's existing pattern of local imports within methods by moving the regex compilation directly into the `_looks_compound` method itself.", "Checking whether adding a module-level `import re` would create any naming conflicts with existing local imports like `import re as _re` inside functions \u2014 but since they're in different scopes, there's no collision risk. Now I need to remove the `consecutive_no_progress` and related constants that ", "Now removing the round-based \"no-progress\" nudge/park mechanism from", "_act_observe", "I need to remove a couple of blocks from the code\u2014the comment and variable initialization around lines 862-873, plus the actual check logic from 917-940\u2014while keeping the `save_verify_failures` initialization and updating the gate comment at line 887-888 that currently references the no-progress hin", "Now removing the actual no-progress check block:", "I need to verify whether `expectation_met` is referenced anywhere else in the code after the block I'm considering removing, since it's still part of the schema and used for context feedback elsewhere\u2014though I notice the later \"WHAT YOU EXPECTED TO HAPPEN\" section actually uses `last_expected_effect", "The local variable `expectation_met` is only used within its own block and doesn't affect anything downstream, so I can safely remove those lines. The schema field with the same