with existing local imports like `import re as _re` inside functions \u2014 but since they're in different scopes, there's no collision risk. Now I need to remove the `consecutive_no_progress` and related constants that ", "Now removing the round-based \"no-progress\" nudge/park mechanism from", "_act_observe", "I need to remove a couple of blocks from the code\u2014the comment and variable initialization around lines 862-873, plus the actual check logic from 917-940\u2014while keeping the `save_verify_failures` initialization and updating the gate comment at line 887-888 that currently references the no-progress hin", "Now removing the actual no-progress check block:", "I need to verify whether `expectation_met` is referenced anywhere else in the code after the block I'm considering removing, since it's still part of the schema and used for context feedback elsewhere\u2014though I notice the later \"WHAT YOU EXPECTED TO HAPPEN\" section actually uses `last_expected_effect", "The local variable `expectation_met` is only used within its own block and doesn't affect anything downstream, so I can safely remove those lines. The schema field with the same name is still important for the model's self-reflection and is referenced in the prompt, but that's separate from this loc", "Now fixing the stale module-level comment referencing the removed mechanism:", "Time to verify that controller.py, pulse.py, and the new office_prefs.py module all compile correctly.", "I should check that those removed variables don't have any lingering references elsewhere in the codebase.", "Clean. Let's quickly sanity-test the new", "_looks_compound", "heuristic and the registry fix logic before restarting the live app.", "All heuristic cases pass. Now testing the registry fix logic directly (dry-run style, checking it's idempotent and safe).", "The first call set the value correctly and the second call did nothing, which confirms the idempotency is working as expected. Now I need to check what the actual registry value shows.", "The registry fix is confirmed working and idempotent, so I'll create a task for this work, restart Pulse, and run the live Word test again while maintaining the same logging and flow tracking. First I need to close any open Word windows to start fresh, then restart Pulse and execute the test.", "All four changes verified individually. Now restarting Pulse and re-running the live Word test with the same logging discipline as before.", "I should clean up any leftover test files from previous attempts\u2014specifically checking if pulse_word_test.docx exists on the Desktop or OneDrive and removing it so the test starts fresh without any stale files interfering with the save logic.", "No existing file to worry about, so I can start fresh. Time to launch Pulse through run.bat.", "I need to check the end of the log file to see if there's a more recent \"Pulse is running\" message beyond the 00:43:37 timestamp, and verify whether the PreferCloudSaveLocations setting is still being logged.", "I'm trying to figure out which restart I'm actually looking at\u2014the timestamp from 00:43:37 could be from my earlier manual test of the office_prefs module, but that wouldn't have produced the \"Pulse is running\" log line since it was just a standalone script. So I must have actually restarted pulse.p", "The Pulse instance restarted cleanly at 00:43:20 AM, which matches the log confirmation, so I'm working with a fresh baseline of 1051 lines. Now I'll run the Word test command to see what happens.", "Confirmed fresh instance running. Sending the Word test now.", "Watching now \u2014 will report as events come in.", "I'm noticing that the SEND response immediately used the \"OVERALL GOAL:\" prefix, which suggests the first response already contained a valid task_list\u2014either the initial attempt succeeded with multiple tasks, or my corrective retry worked. Let me review the full first response to confirm whether tas", "The validation and re